<!-- Methodology -->
<section class="methodology">
  <div class="methodology-inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">How this calculator works</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Most artists run the same rough math in their head: "I sold a print for $25, it cost me about $5, so I made $20." That math is almost always wrong, because it ignores three things that quietly eat your margin — <strong>platform fees</strong>, <strong>shipping to you</strong>, and the <strong>per-print cost difference between small and large runs</strong>.</p>
      <p>This calculator factors all three in. Production cost comes from real Printkeg pricing across <span class="mono">9 product categories</span> and <span class="mono">396 size/paper/quantity combinations</span>. Shipping is tiered based on your run subtotal. Platform fees use current Etsy, Shopify Payments, Square, and standard gallery consignment rates.</p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Platform fee assumptions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Etsy:</strong> 6.5% transaction fee + 3% + $0.25 payment processing + $0.20 listing fee.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own website (Shopify):</strong> 2.9% + $0.30 per transaction (Shopify Payments, Basic plan).</li>
        <li><strong>Convention / In-person:</strong> 2.6% + $0.10 per swipe (Square). Cash sales have no fee.</li>
        <li><strong>Gallery:</strong> 50% consignment, the standard rate for most local galleries.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Shipping (run to artist)</h3>
      <p>Flat-rate tiers based on Printkeg's actual published shipping rates: $10 under $100, $14 from $100–$199, $19 from $200–$299, $29 at $300+. If you're using a different printer, toggle "Using a different printer?" — shipping still applies at these tiers, but you can ignore them if your printer ships free.</p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">What "break-even" means here</h3>
      <p>Break-even is the number of prints you need to sell to cover the cost of the full run (production + shipping). After that quantity, every additional sale is pure profit (minus the per-sale platform fee). So if your break-even is 12 out of 50, the remaining 38 prints are where you actually make money.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

